{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head b0947a298494b47fe8b85ae3e3dd50756ab149be, artifact 208dfcbd4deca48158ca6d10edd1e576e0cc484d.\n\nThe core fold correction is right: satisfaction survives later request retirement; third-party request retirement is distinct from third-party promise retirement; `retiredByAnother` is simpler than carrying actor slices; and workroom-fold@3 correctly invalidates @2 checkpoints. Two blocking surface mismatches and one evidence overclaim remain.\n\n1. The browser hides the state this change exists to reveal. ui/src/lib/work.ts puts `released` in CLOSED_WORK_STATUSES and not in ATTENTION_WORK_STATUSES. WorkView defaults to closed=false and attention=true, so a released promise disappears from the default board even though statusview deliberately keeps it nonterminal and says it is one of the rows readers most need to see. The UI test compounds this by calling released a terminal lifecycle. `released` is attention, not active and not closed: it records a retired promise while the signed request remains live. `revoked` is a closed request outcome but should also be attention-visible because the authority action is intentionally nonterminal in the bounded projection. Pin both through the rendered default-filter path, not only the array literal.\n\nDo not describe released as wanting a “new performer.” The request remains addressed to the same signed actor; what it can take is a replacement promise from that addressed actor, unless the request itself is replaced. The current wording appears in work-loop.md and statusview comments.\n\n2. The profile test does not provide the forward guard its comments, commit, artifact and review request claim. Asserting the literal @3 proves this change did not leave @2; a future fold-output change that leaves @3 untouched still passes. The simplest honest repair is to keep it as a one-change regression pin and remove the claim that it fails on every future contract movement. If a forward guard is wanted, bind a sufficiently broad stable projection fingerprint/golden to the profile and state the limits plainly. Also update statusview/view.go's “exactly the statuses the fold emits” comment, which still lists the old eight.\n\n3. The four behavior pages changed on this head are not re-anchored. work-loop.md, gs/status.md, mcp/work.md and mcp/status.md still cite old behavior artifacts, including retired or stale ones, while this head changes the fold, bounded projections and MCP schema. Re-anchor each changed page to the current behavior artifact(s) as AGENTS.md requires; because that changes the head, state the corresponding exact artifacts and request a fresh review.\n\nLane judgement: released belongs in attention, not actionable. The old promise is gone; classifying its historical row as active would double-count when a replacement promise exists. The live request still needs a replacement promise, and the attention row is the truthful prompt until the projection grows a separate open-row representation.\n\nArchitecture: affected layers are the Workroom application interpreter/fold, projections and queries, and CLI/MCP/UI surfaces. The interpreter compatibility axis intentionally moves @2 to @3; the kernel, Nexus and application-family contracts are unchanged. The exact head preserves architecture.md's contract, so that page itself need not change. Security review found no new trust-boundary issue. Simplification review supports the boolean retirement map and the two distinct statuses; the smaller remaining fix is to align all surfaces with the one lifecycle matrix rather than introduce another status or lane.\n\nExact-head verification passed: go test -count=1 ./internal/workroom ./internal/statusview ./cmd/gitseq-mcp; npm test --prefix ui; focused go vet; make ui-check; git diff --check. The worktree remained clean.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:208dfcbd4deca48158ca6d10edd1e576e0cc484d","hea